course: dictionary
lesson: 13785
title: Meaning from within
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words tell you what a thing is like inside, not what it is used for or what it costs. Intrinsically is one of these words. It means by its own nature, in itself.
explain: «The diamond is intrinsically hard, whoever owns it.» The owner does not change how hard the diamond is. The clue is whoever owns it: the hardness comes from the diamond itself, not from anything added from outside.
explain: «The painting is intrinsically beautiful, not just famous.» Here the words not just famous are the clue. They tell you the beauty belongs to the painting, and fame has nothing to do with it. To check, take out intrinsically and ask whether the meaning has changed. If it has, the word is doing real work.
fact: Intrinsically means by its own nature, or in itself.
fact: «Intrinsically good» means good deep down, in its very nature.
fact: Intrinsically is a formal word, often used in essays, exams and discussions about right and wrong.
fact: In money, a coin's intrinsic value is the metal it is made from, not the number printed on it.
fact: When you see intrinsically, look for a clue such as not because of, or whatever happens outside it.
exercise: «The gold ring is intrinsically valuable, because gold itself is precious.» What does intrinsically mean here? | *By its own nature, in itself | Only on special days | Very quickly | Because someone famous wore it
exercise: In money, a coin's intrinsic value is | The number printed on it | *The metal it is made from | The price a shop sets for it | The picture on its front
exercise: «The lesson was intrinsically interesting, not interesting only because of the prize.» Which phrase shows the reason is inside the lesson? | *not interesting only because of the prize | was | lesson was | the prize
test: «Honesty is intrinsically good.» What does intrinsically mean here? | Good for a short time | Good only on Fridays | Good because someone rewards you | *Good in its own nature, deep down
test: Ali says, «The toy is intrinsically fun, not fun only because it cost a lot.» What does the not only because part tell us? | The toy is broken | The toy is cheap | *The fun comes from the toy itself | Ali does not like the toy
exam: A student writes, «The poem is intrinsically moving.» Which idea does intrinsically add? | The poem was sold for a high price | The poem was written in a hurry | The poem is very long | *The poem moves us because of its own nature, not because of anything added from outside
